le.org';\n```\n\n**Problems**\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` in the WHERE clause is not sargable.** Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `users.email` from being used. The database has to scan every row in `users` and apply `lower()` to each one. This is the biggest issue. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create a functional index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. The query then works as written.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as `citext` in Postgres or a `_ci` collation in MySQL. Then you can write `u.email = 'a@example.org'` and use a normal index.\n   - Store emails already normalized to lowercase, enforce that with a constraint, and compare directly.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.** That widens the rows, adds network and memory cost, and prevents index-only scans. It can also cause trouble if both tables have columns with the same name (`id`, `created_at`, and so on), because the duplicate names are ambiguous or one silently overwrites the other in many clients. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **Index on `orders.user_id`.** I can't see the schema, so I can't tell whether `orders.user_id` is indexed. Without an index, the join becomes a full scan of `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of it. Check that `orders (user_id)` has an index. It's often not automatic, because foreign key constraints don't create one in Postgres.\n\n4. **No `LIMIT` or `ORDER BY`.** A user with many orders returns all of them. If this feeds a UI or an API, add pagination, for example `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT 50`. A composite index on `orders (user_id, created_at)` would support that.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (Postgres-style, adjust column names to your schema):\n\n```sql\n-- CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n-- CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_created_at_idx ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC);\n\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nIf you can share the schema, existing indexes and the database engine, I can be more specific. `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` output would confirm which of these problems actually matters in your case.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}